module

public import Rule30Open.Columns.WordTraps

/-!
# Traps for 101 blocks of period 14, from a wall of period fourteen carried leftward

The natural scan of periods 13 and 14 finds that the commonest traps of 16 cells at period 14, of 384, 225 and 5,269
states, are walls: the closures of the true windows of the white right half driven by `00011010111101`,
`00011100101011` and `00000000000001` at step 12,000, whose chains become periodic in the distance, all three in the
background of 728 cells. Carried leftward up to 103 columns, the wall of 225 states gives traps to 101 blocks of period
14, every block of period 14 among the columns of that background; the kit excludes none of them. The kernel checks
each trap in its own declaration.

* `fo0_<distance>`, `fo0_<distance>_check`: the traps, each passing the check of [WordTraps](WordTraps.md).
* `fourteenTraps`, `fourteenTraps_check`, `fourteenTraps_blocks`: the list, its check and its blocks.
* `fourteen_absorption_excludes`: for each of them, if every finitely supported right half driven by the block enters
  its trap, the center column does not eventually repeat the block.

The tables come from `python -m computations.lock_chain --lean-walls 14`. The natural-language argument is
`FourteenTraps.md`, beside this file.
-/

@[expose] public section

-- Sequential elaboration: the kernel checks would otherwise run in parallel and stack their memory.
set_option Elab.async false

namespace Rule30Open

open Ruliology.CellularAutomata

